Generative AI and Basketball

In my personal opinion basketball has always been about feel. Dribbling the ball, passing to teammates, all the decision making is all about feel, instinct and understanding the game of basketball aside from just the analytics. But in todays game of basketball we are seeing the power of generative AI and how much it can impact not only the statistical and analytical side but also the playing side.

When you hear the two being talked about, initially you wouldn’t think they belong in the same sentence together. But Generative AI is all about patterns, language, prediction and precision. But as the game grows and evolves, especially with the way content, strategy, and training are changing; it’s clear that AI isn’t just on the sidelines anymore. It’s in the gym,film room, in the front office, and even on our phones. And what’s most interesting is that it doesn’t seem like a gimmick. This is the next step of greatness in understanding and really experiencing what basketball is.

A while ago, watching film was the biggest advantage for teams. Coaches, players and even higher ups would sit there for way too long, breaking down every single play on a VHS. Trying to give tips on what they are doing wrong, too many dribbles, missed a defensive assignment etc. But also watching video of their upcoming opponents, their tendencies, their strengths and weaknesses all of that. The littlest things that you would have to watch meticulously. But now there are so many programs that just do that for you. That have generative AI built into the systems and programs. There is one that is called Synergy Sports Editor, this application can take a game and categorize every play into a type of play, which players were involved, what was the outcome and what part of the game it was from.

Another side of things is how it can predict. AI models trained on thousands of games can identify patterns no human ever could. They can predict actions before they happen, recognize how spacing shifts a defense, that can help to create a plan and choose specific plays that will benefit the team. Tools like Synergy (discussed above), Second Spectrum, and proprietary AI systems developed by NBA teams are used to run simulations, analyze every step a player takes, and break down every decision. Like who to guard, when to trap, where to trap and even when to go under a screen. That’s not to say coaches are out of a job. It’s more like they’re getting new tools. These tools give coaches a second set of eyes, ones that don’t miss a moment, and aren’t affected by any outside factors.This results in smarter game plans, more applicable strategies, and an overall deeper understanding of the game of basketball.

The part that I can relate to and speak to the most is the player development side of things. What AI is able to do is so beneficial in so many ways. Like imagine being a high school player trying to improve your jumper. Twenty years ago you’d go to the gym shoot a hundred free throws, maybe have a coach or parent record you on their phone. Today, you can feed your shooting mechanics into a computer vision model and get feedback instantly. At Penn we have something called a NOAH which is a technology through XBOX and it sits at the top of the gym and any shot that is shot on a specific basket it calls out your release angle. While not all shots should be the same there is a general area where most people should be shooting. So when shooting on your own and trying to make real time adjustments without stopping every time to check a camera you just hear it tell you. It it one of the most important things I have had in my career.

AI can also create drills and workouts to help minimize your weaknesses. It can really understand your performance over time, and even simulate what your play style would look like in a specific program system. It’s like having a trainer who knows you better than you do. This is a great tool for helping players looking to go to the next level and grow. It honestly could be a difference maker.

Even in the pros, this stuff is taking hold. Some teams are using generative AI to simulate matchups before they happen. It is like being able to play out a game between you and your next opponent. Then train based on what the AI noticed. It’s a great way of preparation but of course there is no easy shortcuts as at the end of the day there is 10 players and up to around 30,000 fans and that is something the AI can never replicate.

For all the tech, the numbers, the code, and the simulations, there’s still something that can never be replicated. AI can tell you what the most efficient shot is, but it can’t always explain why someone takes the tougher one. It can’t model the moment when a player hits a game-winner after missing ten straight. It can’t feel the energy shift when a bench player starts to take over. This is the part of basketball that makes it so special. Brings in the emotion and the unexpected while fans cheer their hearts out for the team they love. Of course AI can assist many things but it can never replicate what really happens in the game of basketball.

Basketball and generative AI may seem like they don't belong together at first glance. But the more you look at how the game is played, taught, studied, and shared, the more it makes sense. AI isn’t changing what basketball is, it’s changing how we can get the most out of it and get into things that humans might not be able to process. It’s giving coaches sharper tools, players deeper insights, and fans new ways to engage. In the end the only thing that matters is continuing to use these tools in a positive way to benefit and enhance the game of basketball and all it’s people involved. 
